	{
		"name": "execute_sql",
		"title": "Execute SQL atomically",
		"description": "Run an ordered atomic SQL batch against lix_ref (@handle/slug or UUID; no open_lix needed), optionally branch_id, or context alone for its pinned branch. If a statement fails, the complete batch is rolled back; a returned result is already persisted, read back only if the outcome is unknown or validation is part of the task. UI hosts show a card; summarize, do not list files.\n\nThe one write path: one call per coherent change, its creates, updates, moves, copies, deletes and directory changes in order. checkpoint: true marks a milestone (saving needs no checkpoint); last_seen_commit_id (read_files' commit_id or a batch's commit.after) refuses the batch if a file it touches changed since. Upsert: `INSERT INTO lix_file (path, content) VALUES ($1, CAST($2 AS BYTEA)) ON CONFLICT (path) DO UPDATE SET content = excluded.content` (metadata: lixcol_metadata); parents are created. Move/rename: `UPDATE lix_file SET path = $1 WHERE path = $2` keeps the id, so links survive; an occupied target fails the batch. Copy+delete makes a new id; never move that way. Delete: `DELETE FROM lix_file WHERE path = $1`. Directories likewise on lix_directory (no trailing slash: /skills, not /skills/). Example: [upsert /gtm/plan.md, move /gtm/a.md to /gtm/archive/a.md, upsert a redirect at /gtm/a.md], checkpoint: true. Errors carry statement_index and an outcome; never blindly retry unknown or committed.",
